WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:06.340
What did you learn about risk at a whorehouse?

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:09.560
It's a very risky job, especially if you're
working the illegal market

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:12.020
there's always a risk that your client could
be the police

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:13.880
or just a deranged lunatic who will kill you.

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:19.820
Allison Schrager is the author of An Economist
Walk Into A Brothel

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:24.680
a fascinating dive into how different communities
assess risk and what we can learn from that

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:28.380
including sex workers at the Moonlite Bunny
Ranch in Nevada.

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:32.000
I went to the brothel to see how the legal
market manages risk

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.960
'cause the legal market provides a fairly
risk free space to do sex work

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:41.040
where the women have security, where they
don't have to worry about screening their clients

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:44.340
and they go through thorough background checks
and disease screenings

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:45.680
and have a lot of support.

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:49.760
Their risk is minimized and then also the
risk for the johns is also minimized?

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:53.240
Exactly and that's sort of an interesting
play on pimp I guess

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:57.300
Dennis Hof when he was alive loved sort of
reclaiming the expression pimp.

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:59.380
Because when I think of a pimp I think of
this guy

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:03.220
who's pushing these women into these very dangerous
situations and then taking all the money.

00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:06.800
So we call that in finance an unsensible risk,

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:10.920
you generally, in finance people who take
more risk are supposed to get more reward

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:13.360
and you know a market isn't functioning properly

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:16.070
when the people who take the biggest risks
don't get the biggest reward

00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:20.420
which happens often in crime because they're
sort of messed up markets for a lot of reasons.

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:25.400
So what's interesting about what Dennis does
is he actually makes his spread, you know,

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:28.400
what the johns pay and what the women have
to pay

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:31.580
to minimize risk or reduce risk for everyone
involved in the transaction.

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:34.840
He is sort of a pioneer in the world of sex
work.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:38.380
He was running some sort of business with
time shares

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:42.160
and just had a lot of friends in the porn
industry and spent time in brothels

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:46.390
and had this idea that you could approach
sex work the way you approach time shares.

00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:48.640
It's like a total ripoff is what you're saying.

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:52.320
I think it's a fair transaction, you know,
you're not locked in like you are with a time share

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:54.880
you're not stuck seeing these sex workers
year after year

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:58.360
and it's not like you then have to sell your
time with them somewhere else.

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:02.000
But the idea is that you are effectively renting
someone,

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:04.680
renting a service I guess is a nicer way to
think about it.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:07.500
Nevada has a long history with legal sex work.

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:11.620
Before, you know, they were kinda dingy sort
of depressing places.

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:14.820
They're not like luxury spas, the women there
were, you know,

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:18.620
having to be there for hours or days at a
time where they weren't allowed to leave.

00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:23.120
There were set prices, they had to, no control
over who they were servicing.

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:26.340
So this idea that he was going to make them
all independent businesswomen

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:29.360
and that they would negotiate every transaction
on their own terms

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:34.709
only have sex with people they wanted to and
set the prices themselves.

00:02:34.709 --> 00:02:36.750
To meet with the world's most interesting
risk takers,

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:40.100
Schrager traveled the country to study horse
breeders, military commanders,

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:42.900
professional poker players and Hollywood paparazzi.

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:45.260
On Hawaii's north shore she found herself
attending the

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:48.580
Annual Big Wave Risk Assessment Safety S ummit

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:53.140
where she found that surfers and financial
risk advisers have a lot in common.

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:55.920
Their leader is this guy named Brian Keaulana

00:02:55.930 --> 00:02:59.490
who had this idea that risk is something that
could be managed

00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:01.900
which is the whole ethos of financial economics.

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:06.320
So he had the same idea and he's been studying
risk science with the military

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:09.720
and had this idea that they would regularly
have these summits

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:12.500
where they would discuss risk science but
for big wave surfers.

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:16.460
And they really discuss a lot of the same
things we do in pension conferences.

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:21.540
Different ways you can minimize risk, manage
risk, take risks in smarter ways.

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:27.140
For instance waves travel in sets, so you'll,
like big waves will run in a pack of five.

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:32.440
In finance we'd call this a hedge, which is
taking less risk but still getting some reward

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:36.500
sort of calibrating it just so, so you never
want to take an early wave in a set,

00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:40.000
because if you wipe out there'll be four or
five large waves barreling on you

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:42.380
so what you want to do is take a wave in the
fourth or fifth set,

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:44.620
in a way that might be a smaller wave,

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:46.540
just so you have less risks.

00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:48.060
They talk about things like that.

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:53.120
These guys are not necessarily adrenaline
junkies, they, what they do is risky

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:55.000
but they're not big risk takers.

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:58.920
No in fact there's almost something very intellectual
about the way they take risk

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:02.720
it's, you know I was talking to Greg Long
who's a very well known big wave surfer

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:07.590
about how it's almost this intellectual endeavor
of here's this huge force of matter

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:10.849
how can I do this in a very intellectual way
and minimize the risk

00:04:10.849 --> 00:04:13.440
and still be able to do this activity.

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:16.500
The markets that are working well in your
book are kind of,

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:21.900
they're free and open where a lot of different
participants, you know, kind of work through things

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:24.820
and certain norms that kind of inscribe.

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:28.240
On the one hand that means when people from
outside and industry come in and say

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:29.380
you guys are doing it all wrong

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:33.720
I'm going to bring my, you know, my outsider
knowledge to bear on this.

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:36.800
They don't typically work very well, or it
doesn't last very long

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:41.520
but then also regulators often come in and
figure they can tame a market.

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:43.580
Is there something going on there about risk

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:47.440
that regulators and kind of neophytes both
dismiss?

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:49.340
Yeah for sure and I mean I did that too.

00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:53.400
Because I would go into these markets, I don't
have any expertise in surfing or horse breeding

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:54.610
and I would always come in with

00:04:54.610 --> 00:04:58.400
'well this is how we do it in finance and
have you thought about doing options on studs?'

00:04:58.400 --> 00:04:59.640
and everyone's always like

00:04:59.650 --> 00:05:01.430
'no of course not, this is not how it works.'

00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:03.220
and of course they were always right.

00:05:03.220 --> 00:05:08.080
But the regulation's hard because every community
has this very strong libertarian streak.

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:11.900
Like the big discussion at the big wave surf
conference was systemic risk

00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:16.160
so systemic risk is that you take your big
institution, you take a big risk,

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:17.680
and you bring down the whole system.

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:20.460
So this is the big debate in big wave surfing
as they, you know,

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:25.300
as technology be itll jet skis or inflatable vests,
encourage people to take on more risk

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:27.800
and that diverts rescue resources to other
people

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:30.820
or you have water crashing into other people

00:05:30.820 --> 00:05:33.820
or, you know, it's a safety risk for people
to have to rescue them.

00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:36.000
So it's in a sense a systemic risk.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:40.100
And so they're constantly debating who should
be able to buy safety equipment

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:43.480
and who should be surfing big waves.

00:05:43.480 --> 00:05:46.860
And I was like 'well you know we have these
discussions at pension conferences

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:49.600
and it sounds like you're asking for some
level of regulation.'

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:54.960
You know, how, should you be like, should
you be licensed to be a big wave surfer?

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:57.750
Like the same way you're licensed to be a
scuba diver.

00:05:57.750 --> 00:06:00.620
And this as I said like, they're all pretty
liberal about most things.

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:06.230
They're like 'oh the big wave vest, the inflatable
vest company that sells vests to anyone'

00:06:06.230 --> 00:06:09.260
which they find very offensive because it
means more risk for everyone

00:06:09.260 --> 00:06:10.860
'they're just about the money.'

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:13.040
But once I was like 'well do you think you
should be regulated

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:15.300
do you think that big wave surfing should
be licensed?'

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:18.930
They're like 'no, no we need to keep the government
out of big wave surfing.'

00:06:18.930 --> 00:06:20.460
They were very emphatic about that.

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:22.320
What is the big takeaway that you,

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:25.580
because the book is also on a certain level
is a self help book

00:06:25.580 --> 00:06:28.280
or you know a personal finance book, let me
put it that way.

00:06:28.280 --> 00:06:33.560
Yeah personal finance gets a bad rap, you
know, especially in academic finance.

00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:36.780
But I think it's a bigger message than just
personal finance

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:39.660
which is that people should feel comfortable
taking more risks.

00:06:39.660 --> 00:06:42.780
We don't really give people the tools to feel
comfortable with risk taking.

00:06:42.780 --> 00:06:45.660
But you really do need to take risks to make
your life move forward.

00:06:45.660 --> 00:06:51.100
In any aspect of your life, if it's your relationship,
if it's your job, anything

00:06:51.100 --> 00:06:54.860
is to help people understand that there is
a way of taking risk

00:06:54.860 --> 00:06:58.140
where they can do stuff to protect themselves
if things go wrong,

00:06:58.140 --> 00:06:59.640
there aren't any guarantees,

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:02.100
but they can definitely increase the odds
things will work out for them.

